Its \(K_{sp}= [\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}][\mathrm{OH^-}]^{3}=4\times10^{-38}\) (approx.).  
The concentration of \(\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}\) obtained from dissolving 0.1 g in 0.1 L is  

\[
n(\mathrm{Fe(OH)_3})=\frac{0.1\ \text{g}}{M_{\mathrm{Fe(OH)_3}}}
      =\frac{0.1}{106.87}\approx9.36\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}
\]
\[
c(\mathrm{Fe^{3+}})=\frac{9.36\times10^{-4}}{0.10}\approx9.4\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}
\]

To keep the solid dissolved we must suppress the \(\mathrm{OH^-}\) concentration by adding acid.  
From the solubility product

\[
[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \left(\frac{K_{sp}}{[\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}]}\right)^{1/3}
                 =\left(\frac{4\times10^{-38}}{9.4\times10^{-3}}\right)^{1/3}
                 \approx1.7\times10^{-12}\ \text{M}
\]

